CAT = "nzfkStability"; SEG = "STB"
# Forklift physics: centre of gravity, stability triangle and pyramid, mast tilt, turning, rear swing, axle loading.
Q = [
('Most counterbalanced forklifts rest on three suspension points. What shape do they form?',
 'Stability triangle', ['Stability rectangle', 'Load-moment square', 'Tipping circle'],
 'The two front wheels and the pivot of the rear axle form the stability triangle. The truck stays upright while the combined centre of gravity stays inside it.'),
('When does a counterbalanced forklift tip over?',
 'Its line of action leaves the triangle', ['The load is pressed to the backrest', 'The mast is fully lowered', 'The truck reverses slowly'],
 'Skill standard 40735 describes forklift physics as keeping the combined centre of gravity of truck and load within the wheelbase; outside the stability triangle the truck tips.'),
("Skill standard 40735 lists a 'stability pyramid' too. What does lifting the load do within it?",
 'Raises the combined balance point', ["Lowers the truck's total mass", "Widens the truck's wheelbase", 'Shortens the rated load centre'],
 'Raising the load lifts the combined centre of gravity up the pyramid toward its apex, so smaller sideways or forward forces can tip the truck.'),
("Where does an unladen counterbalanced truck's centre of gravity usually sit?",
 'Toward the rear', ['Right at the fork tips', 'Just above the mast top', 'Directly under the front axle'],
 "The counterweight puts an empty truck's centre of gravity toward the rear. Picking up a load moves the combined point forward toward the front axle."),
('Which axle is the pivot point when a counterbalanced forklift tips forward?',
 'Front axle', ['Rear steer axle', 'Mast pivot', 'Counterweight pin'],
 "The front (drive) wheels are the fulcrum: the load's moment in front of them is balanced by the truck and counterweight behind them."),
("When is a forklift's lateral stability set only by the truck's own centre of gravity?",
 'When it carries no load', ['When the forks are raised', 'When it is turning left', 'When the load is centred'],
 "OSHA's stability appendix notes that with no load, the truck's own centre of gravity is the only factor in its lateral stability; a load adds its weight to the combined point."),
('What does tilting the mast back while travelling with a load do?',
 'Moves the load toward the truck', ['Raises the load centre', 'Shifts weight to the steer wheels only', 'Makes the forks longer'],
 'Back tilt cradles the load against the backrest and moves its centre of gravity toward the truck, improving stability and load security when travelling.'),
('Why is forward tilt with a raised load dangerous?',
 'It moves the load forward', ['It lowers the counterweight', 'It drains the hydraulic fluid', 'It locks the steering'],
 'Tilting forward with a raised load pushes the centre of gravity forward of the front axle, increasing the tipping moment.'),
('Which of these most increases the risk of a sideways tip?',
 'Turning sharply at speed', ['Travelling slowly in a straight line', 'Carrying the load low', 'Sounding the horn at corners'],
 'Turning creates sideways forces, and speed and sharpness of the turn both increase them. A load carried high adds to the risk.'),
('A forklift carrying a raised load brakes hard. What can happen?',
 'It can tip forward', ['It can tip sideways only', 'Its battery can disconnect', 'The mast lowers itself'],
 'Harsh braking with a heavy or raised load adds forward force, which can carry the combined centre of gravity past the front axle.'),
('What is rear end swing?',
 'Counterweight swinging out', ['Mast swaying when raised', 'Load sliding on the forks', 'Rear tyres skidding on ice'],
 'A counterbalanced truck steers with its rear wheels, so the rear swings out wide when turning. People and racking beside the truck can be struck.'),
('An operator turns close to a pedestrian walkway. Which part of the truck is most likely to strike someone?',
 'Rear counterweight', ['Fork tips at the front', 'Overhead guard', 'Front drive wheels'],
 'Rear-wheel steering swings the counterweight out on turns, so a person standing beside the rear quarter can be hit.'),
('What is axle loading?',
 'Weight on each axle', ['Fuel used by each axle', 'Tyre wear per shift', 'Steering lock per turn'],
 'Axle loading is how the weight of truck and load is shared between the axles. A heavy load puts most of the weight on the front axle.'),
('A laden truck must cross a suspended mezzanine floor. Which figure matters most?',
 'Total weight it must carry', ["The truck's top speed", "The mast's free lift height", "The load's packaging type"],
 'The floor must support the truck and load together, concentrated through the front axle when laden. Floors and dock plates should be marked with their rating.'),
('Which load handling choice keeps a forklift most stable when travelling?',
 'Load low and tilted back', ['Load high and tilted back', 'Load low and tilted forward', 'Load high and level'],
 'Carrying the load just clear of the ground with the mast tilted back keeps the combined centre of gravity low and toward the truck.'),
('A forklift is driven across a slope instead of straight up or down. What is the risk?',
 'Tipping over sideways', ['Battery overcharging', 'Forks bending downward', 'Mast chains slackening'],
 'Travelling or turning across a gradient tilts the truck sideways and can carry the combined centre of gravity outside the stability triangle.'),
('Why should operators avoid potholes and loose material?',
 'A wheel drop can tip it', ['They wear out the seatbelt', 'They drain the battery faster', 'They confuse the horn sensor'],
 'Running a wheel into a pothole tilts the truck sideways, reducing lateral stability; small-wheeled trucks are most affected.'),
('What does a side shift do?',
 'Moves forks left or right', ['Tilts the mast sideways', 'Turns the rear wheels', 'Lifts the counterweight'],
 'Side shift moves the carriage and forks sideways to line up a load. 40735 lists use of side shift among the operating parameters.'),
('Why should a load be centred again after using side shift at height?',
 'It cuts sideways stability', ['It saves hydraulic fluid', 'It keeps the horn working', 'It stops the forks rusting'],
 "Side-shifting moves the load's centre of gravity sideways. Travelling with it off-centre reduces lateral stability, so recentre before moving."),
('What do fork positioners adjust?',
 'Space between the forks', ['Height of the overhead guard', 'Angle of the seat', 'Tyre pressure'],
 'Fork positioners move the tines closer or further apart to suit the load. 40735 lists them among the operating parameters.'),
('Why should the forks be spread as wide as the load allows?',
 'To support it evenly', ['To raise the rated load centre', 'To lengthen the wheelbase', 'To speed up the lift'],
 'Forks spread under the load support it evenly and stop it tipping off one side, which keeps the combined centre of gravity central.'),
('A tall, narrow load sways when travelling. What does that do to the combined centre of gravity?',
 'It moves it about', ['It locks it in place', 'It lowers it to the floor', 'It moves it under the seat'],
 'A swaying load shifts the combined centre of gravity forward and sideways with each swing, so travel slowly with the load low and secured.'),
('What does raising an empty set of forks to full height do to stability?',
 'Makes the truck easier to tip', ['Has no effect without a load', 'Lowers the centre of gravity', 'Widens the stability triangle'],
 'Even unladen, a raised mast and carriage move the combined centre of gravity up, so the truck tips more easily on slopes and turns.'),
('What is the state of a truck whose load moment exactly equals its own resisting moment?',
 'Balanced, at the tipping point', ['Safe with room to spare', 'Already tipping backward', 'Unaffected by turning'],
 'Like a see-saw, equal moments balance. Any extra load, forward tilt or braking then tips the truck forward, so a load at that limit leaves no margin.'),
('A 900 kg load sits 1.2 m in front of the front axle. What is its moment about the axle?',
 '1,080 kg m', ['750 kg m', '900 kg m', '1,200 kg m'],
 'Moment = 900 kg x 1.2 m = 1,080 kg m. Dividing instead of multiplying gives 750.'),
("The truck's counterweight side gives 4,000 kg m of resisting moment. Which load moment would tip it forward?",
 '4,400 kg m', ['3,200 kg m', '3,800 kg m', '2,000 kg m'],
 'The truck tips forward when the load moment exceeds the resisting moment. Only 4,400 kg m is above 4,000 kg m.'),
('Why does a forklift slow down before turning with a load?',
 'To cut sideways force', ['To save the brake pads', 'To let the forks lower', 'To keep the battery cool'],
 'Sideways force on a turn rises steeply with speed. Slowing first keeps the combined centre of gravity inside the stability triangle.'),
('Why should a restrained operator not jump clear of a tipping counterbalanced truck?',
 'They can be trapped under it', ['The truck then tips faster', 'The seatbelt stops working', 'The forks will rise sharply'],
 "HSE's lift truck code says restraints are fitted because an operator thrown or jumping out can be trapped between the truck and the ground. Stay in the seat with the restraint fastened."),
]
